00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,800
9 · Research: RQ1 to RQ6 and ambulance reach

48
00:05:11,316 --> 00:05:17,453
The Research page: every result with its city, dataset, tables, figures and sources.
[Each number is read from a saved results file with its evidence]

49
00:05:17,453 --> 00:05:41,019
RQ2 · Forecast-aware routing (Los Angeles twin): a perfect view of the next hour helps; our forecasts do not yet.
[SUMO closed loop; paired seeds]

50
00:05:41,019 --> 00:06:06,221
RQ1 · Forecasting: Los Angeles models are far better than "no change"; in Dhaka only slightly, and not an hour ahead.
[METR-LA, PEMS-BAY (DCRNN release), DhaTraX; BasicTS, Chronos, TimesFM]

51
00:06:06,221 --> 00:06:21,237
RQ3 · Ambulance alert in Dhaka (labelled RQ2 on this page, the older numbering): the runs behind the result.
[Eclipse SUMO, ITS-G5 and C-V2X delivery models]

52
00:06:21,237 --> 00:06:57,036
RQ3 · At Dhaka's density the alert saves a median 26 s on a 346 s trip, but 4 of 10 runs are slower than the siren alone.
[make-way rule v2; signal pre-emption adapted from atsc-rl]

53
00:06:57,036 --> 00:07:13,605
Supporting · Ambulance reach: share of Dhaka within 10, 20, 30 minutes of an emergency hospital.
[Valhalla isochrones (gis-ops/valhalla-app approach)]

54
00:07:13,605 --> 00:07:19,089
RQ6 · Crash detection: the WreckWatch rule on synthetic traces: a normal ride and a hard brake do not trigger it; a crash-like jolt does.
[WreckWatch (White et al., 2011)]

55
00:07:19,089 --> 00:07:32,088
RQ5 · Weak signal: with the trip saved in advance the route appears sooner on slow networks.
[Bartendr (MobiCom 2010), BreadCrumbs (MobiCom 2008); headless Chrome throttling]

56
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,100
